      "level": 1,
      "content": "Sorting Algorithm"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Suppose we have an ArrayList with elements 999, 212, 333. And we want them to be sorted in ascending order."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Instead of writing whole coding logic to sort it, we can simply use the sort() method to sort them."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "content": "1import java.util.ArrayList;\n2import java.util.Collections;\n3\n4class Main {\n5    public static void main(String[] args) {\n6\n7        // create an ArrayList\n8        ArrayList<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();\n9\n10        // add elements\n11        numbers.add(999);\n12        numbers.add(212);\n13        numbers.add(333);\n14        System.out.println(\"Unsorted ArrayList: \" + numbers);\n15\n16        // sort the ArrayList\n17        Collections.sort(numbers);\n18        System.out.println(\"Sorted ArrayList: \" + numbers);\n19\n20    }\n21}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "Unsorted ArrayList: [999, 212, 333]\nSorted ArrayList: [212, 333, 999]"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, we have used the Collections.sort() method to sort all the elements of the arraylist."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "To use this method, we have imported the Collections package."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "content": "import java.util.Collections;"
    },
